# Working in this repo

This is a course project. The point is that *you* understand the service and can
defend the decisions in it, not that the code reaches a finished state fastest.
Use the AI here the way you would use a colleague who types quickly: ask it to
explain, to review, to try an approach — then read what came back and decide.

## What the service is

FastAPI service that triages customer support tickets for Alton Energy. One
endpoint, `POST /v1/triage`. Per request: classify urgency (model) → look up the
account (external tool) → retrieve help articles (embeddings) → draft an answer
(model). Start reading at `app/main.py`, then `app/services/triage.py`.

## Rules for changes

- Model calls go through `app/services/model_client.py`. Never call a provider
  directly from `triage.py` or `retrieval.py`.
- Two providers: `mock` (offline, free, deterministic) and `openrouter` (real).
  Selected by `MODEL_PROVIDER`. Any change must keep both working.
- Load testing runs on the mock. Do not benchmark against a real model — the
  numbers move on their own and it costs money.
- Never hardcode an API key. It comes from `.env`, which is gitignored.
- Every performance or reliability claim needs evidence from
  `scripts/load_probe.py` or `scripts/analyze_logs.py`, not an assertion.
- Keep `pytest` green.

## Things in this repo that look wrong and are not

- The endpoint is `async def` but the calls inside it block. That is the Sprint 1
  problem. Do not "fix" it before the sprint asks you to.
- `retrieval.search()` re-embeds the whole corpus every request. That is the
  Sprint 2 problem.
- Nothing handles a failing dependency. That is the Sprint 3 problem.
- Mock latency values in `config.py` are deliberately slow. Lowering them is not
  a performance improvement.
